          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why does acid reflux tend to happen after meals?",
          "answer": "Acid reflux often follows meals because a full, slowly emptying stomach gives acid more chances to rise. The sources I searched explain some of the factors but do not describe the underlying muscle mechanisms in detail.\n\n**What the sources say:**\n- **Large or late meals** are listed among the common contributors to reflux (condition-gerd). The reference notes that a heavy late dinner can provoke symptoms more reliably than the same food eaten at midday. The effect depends as much on how long the food stays in the stomach as on the food itself (lifestyle-gerd-diet).\n- **Fatty meals slow stomach emptying**, which extends the period when reflux can happen (lifestyle-gerd-diet).\n- **Carbonated drinks** add gas that stretches the stomach directly (lifestyle-gerd-diet).\n- **Alcohol** is described as relaxing the valve between the stomach and the food pipe, and increasing acid exposure (lifestyle-gerd-diet).\n- **Excess body weight, especially around the abdomen**, and a **hiatus hernia** are also listed as contributors (condition-gerd).\n- **Smoking** and **pregnancy** are listed as contributors (condition-gerd).\n- Some **medicines** that relax the lower esophageal sphincter can worsen reflux (condition-gerd). I can't say which ones from these sources, and a clinician or pharmacist is the right person to ask about your own medicines.\n\n**What the sources do not cover:** They do not explain in detail how or why the lower esophageal sphincter relaxes after eating, or how stomach volume changes that process. I have not filled that gap from memory.\n\n**Individual triggers:** Guidance usually suggests noting what you eat and when symptoms occur over a couple of weeks. Most people find two or three real triggers, which differ from person to person. Cutting out everything on the common trigger lists is not usually recommended, because the evidence that it helps is weak (lifestyle-gerd-diet).\n\nIf symptoms continue despite these changes, guidance says to review timing and any medicines with a clinician, who may arrange further tests (guideline-gerd-initial-management-and-alarm-features).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
